{
  "dom_id": "step-q72916",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Determine the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-12y+35=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-12y+35 & =0 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2(0)-12y+35 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-12y+35 & =0 \\\\ (y-6)^{2}-36+35 & =0 \\\\ (y-6)^{2} & =1 \\\\ y-6 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ y & =6\\pm 1 \\\\ y & =5,\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,5)$ and $(0,7).$",
